{"title":"2012 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 12A Problems\">2012 AMC 12A #14</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Problems\">2012 AMC 10A #18</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Looking_at_the_answer_choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Looking at the answer choices)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Areas)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Areas)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Quick_Equilateral_Triangles)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Quick Equilateral Triangles)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The closed curve in the figure is made up of 9 congruent circular arcs each of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/1/3a1f43766bf2f5511df35ab6d4a0b84dbcb6dadb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"22\" height=\"37\" />, where each of the centers of the corresponding circles is among the vertices of a regular hexagon of side 2. What is the area enclosed by the curve?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/3/c431f575740b9b695b706edb44a2fbc2971431e3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm); defaultpen(fontsize(6pt)); dotfactor=4; label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,-1)); dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.4325,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75)); draw(Arc((0,1),0.5,210,-30)); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150)); draw(Arc((-0.865,-0.5),0.5,330,90)); draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30)); [/asy]\" width=\"262\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/0/c306fcbe2d8a4260fd2ef81f005993e228540223.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 2\\pi+6\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 2\\pi+4\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3\\pi+4\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 2\\pi+3\\sqrt{3}+2\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\pi+6\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"656\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/2/7e2c2ecbb0dbbfbcd0f545299c35a19aff45547b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm); defaultpen(fontsize(6pt)); dotfactor=4; label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,-1)); dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.4325,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75)); draw(Arc((0,1),0.5,210,-30)); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150)); draw(Arc((-0.865,-0.5),0.5,330,90)); draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30)); draw((0,1)--(0.865, 0.5)--(0.865,-0.5)--(0,-1)--(-0.865,-0.5)--(-0.865,0.5)--(0,1)); [/asy]\" width=\"262\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>We can draw the hexagon between the centers of the circles, and compute its area. The hexagon is made of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equilateral triangles each with length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so the area is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7e6cfa0c25805523d6ff6d764a5633e6117e03c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 2^2 \\cdot 6=6 \\sqrt{3}.\\]\" width=\"139\" height=\"41\" />\nThen, we add the areas of the three sectors outside the hexagon:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/7/5e7832fd8fe8df1e42094a007f80e9042afa70a4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac 23 \\pi \\cdot 3=2\\pi.\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"37\" />\nWe now subtract the areas of the three sectors inside the hexagon but outside the figure (which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />) to get the area enclosed in the curved figure:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/4/644f9450d66904e1d6bd4326b4e523890c741319.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[6 \\sqrt{3}+2\\pi-\\pi=\\pi+6\\sqrt{3}.\\]\" width=\"203\" height=\"18\" />\nHence, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/9/b9939990b8ebfda9600c6e563f1c3edf6094e7e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\pi+6\\sqrt{3}},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"118\" height=\"35\" /> and we are done.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/f/57f1406b65f9f2e6a365b7356da731780b42cceb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\]\" />\n(Minor edits, made by dbnl.)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Looking_at_the_answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Looking_at_the_answer_choices)\">Solution 2 (Looking at the answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Looking at the answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>After forming the hexagon using the sectors outside the hexagon, we see three sectors left. Each sector has an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/d/95d69c68f960375b5368b628c6a7f2370a36dae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\pi}{3},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"19\" height=\"33\" /> so the three combined make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/b/a2bdc0e6e1032d4974b7dc236c496c37563358d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> Since the side length of the hexagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/1/6e1d1407aac4125d6c453a365eb173d96ebb48e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"17\" /> its area doesn't have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> in it, so we know that the final answer will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/8/f384dbb1cdca5c58763a64920e492975a83ce502.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi + \\text{(area of the hexagon)}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"196\" height=\"18\" /> Looking at the answer choices, the only answer with only one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/3/9637ded1c3fcafb638e9de07271242a795f4043c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"42\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>Also, notice the problem consists of adding and subtracting arcs from a hexagon with an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/7/8772d61f5af0b70f355caf61084162db9b47f34b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Since all of the arcs have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> in them, they will not affect the area of the hexagon, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/7/8772d61f5af0b70f355caf61084162db9b47f34b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />, in the final answer. Thus, the only possible solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/2/55206bce678ac0394d97dc04dd72398df2bdf572.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{E}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"26\" height=\"24\" />. ~Extremelysupercooldude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Areas.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Areas)\">Solution 3 (Areas)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Areas)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As you can see, this diagram looks like a fidget spinner&#160;;). Fidget spinners aside, we need to add stuff to our diagram to make it look easier. In the directions, they were talking about the centers of each arc create a hexagon, so let's add that to our diagram. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/c/e1c65590aa00f1ae1b500b2751da781677dc577d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] defaultpen(fontsize(6pt)); dotfactor=4; label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,-1)); dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.432,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75)); draw(Arc((0,1),0.5,210,-30)); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150)); draw(Arc((-0.865,-0.5),0.5,330,90)); draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30)); draw((0,1)--(0.865, 0.5)--(0.865,-0.5)--(0,-1)--(-0.865,-0.5)--(-0.865,0.5)--(0,1)); [/asy]\" width=\"668\" height=\"618\" />\n</p><p>The side length of the hexagon is 2 and if we plug it in to the area of a regular hexagon formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/0/610530acd444d62c17b0c123baf24556305e3a71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3\\sqrt 3}{2}s^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"43\" /> we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e15d7edb720f42d585c4e0eeef2db57ea036df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Note that the interior angles of a regular hexagon is 120 because of the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d1894e46499b6a3d77af0ae2e49c2cfcda193eb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{180(n-2)}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"40\" /> where n is the number of sides. Knowing that, each sector is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of a circle. This would mean the three sectors inside the hexagon altogether equal a full circle. Knowing that the radius is 1, we can use the area of a circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89ed1c5456915b4e95e0c8a11257ac5ed3d32c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi r^2$\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> and subtract it to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e15d7edb720f42d585c4e0eeef2db57ea036df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Thus we get the total area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/8/f3819457ad07dd893ee1bf9d63bda3205883df10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt 3 - \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"68\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nNotice that we have three sectors exterior to the hexagon. Realize that the central angles of a circle always sum up to 360. Since we know one of the central angles is equal to 120, then we subtract 360 to 120 which gives us 240. Knowing that, each sector is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of a circle and since there is 3 of them, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a8203b555e8b8f4f6e4e3b351f742e17dc6c718c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}*3=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" /> circles. To find the area of those circles, we have to use <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89ed1c5456915b4e95e0c8a11257ac5ed3d32c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi r^2$\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> again, but since there are 2 circles, then it is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/f/b6f1a73df0708c0c9df6c48d28c1db688f12c15f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi r^2$\" width=\"35\" height=\"15\" />, which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/9/709acef3551b886e6754de72d59a88f00660f0d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"22\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Now we have enough information to find the total area, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae5b4668784ca104306962170dac15dc5240f0ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6\\sqrt 3 -\\pi+2\\pi)=\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\pi+6\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"249\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p>~ghfhgvghj10\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Quick_Equilateral_Triangles.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Quick_Equilateral_Triangles)\">Solution 4 (Quick Equilateral Triangles)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Quick Equilateral Triangles)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, realize that if you put 3 points around the tips of the fidget spinner shape, you get a big equilateral triangle, like this:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6ab98c7a046bf32162fed48d0964397591672f1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm); defaultpen(fontsize(6pt)); dotfactor=4;  label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,-1));  dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.4325,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75));  draw(Arc((0,1),0.5,210,-30)); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150)); draw(Arc((-0.865,-0.5),0.5,330,90)); draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30));  // equilateral triangle draw((0,1.5)--(-1.2975,-0.75)--(1.2975,-0.75)--cycle);  [/asy]\" width=\"262\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>This cleanly gives the 9 arcs, and some can be cancelled out. 3 of these arcs make a circle, and each of the 3 arcs are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/1/3a1f43766bf2f5511df35ab6d4a0b84dbcb6dadb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"22\" height=\"37\" />. This means that when 3 of the small arcs are combined, it gives the circumference, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/7/b172a8323d74730fe507b03045746afab2ed10c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\cdot3=2\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"38\" />. This means the radius of the small circles are each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d7d7e6659cac17b89e2678cb85e1d2529b70e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{2}=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"45\" height=\"37\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/1/491e9932fef64fcc798e7fe9175198ffba3e49d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm); defaultpen(fontsize(6pt)); dotfactor=4;  label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,-1));  dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.4325,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75));  draw(Arc((0,1),0.5,210,-30)); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270)); draw(Arc((0.865,-0.5),0.5,90,-150)); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150)); draw(Arc((-0.865,-0.5),0.5,330,90)); draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30));  // equilateral triangle draw((0,1.5)--(-1.2975,-0.75)--(1.2975,-0.75)--cycle);  // complete the top small circle draw(circle((0,1),0.5));  [/asy]\" width=\"262\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>It can be realized that the large equilateral triangle can be divided into 9 smaller equilateral trianlges, and they are inscribed in the small circles, as shown below.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57c19dbf93621a48c659a8a1f8fbee918893900d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(2cm);  pen thinpen = linewidth(0.7); pen thickpen = linewidth(1.2);  defaultpen(thinpen); dotfactor=4;  // circles thinner draw(circle((0,1),0.5), thinpen); draw(circle((-0.865,-0.5),0.5), thinpen); draw(circle((0.865,-0.5),0.5), thinpen);  // side arcs draw(Arc((-0.865,0.5),0.5,-90,30), thinpen); draw(Arc((0.865,0.5),0.5,150,270), thinpen); draw(Arc((0,-1),0.5,30,150), thinpen); // outer triangle draw((0,1.5)--(-1.2975,-0.75)--(1.2975,-0.75)--cycle, thickpen);  // inner horizontal lines draw((-0.4325,0.75)--(0.4325,0.75), thickpen); draw((-0.865,0)--(0.865,0), thickpen);  // inner triangle lines slightly thicker draw((-0.4325,0.75)--(0,0), thickpen); draw((0.4325,0.75)--(0,0), thickpen);  draw((-0.4325,-0.75)--(0,0), thickpen); draw((0.4325,-0.75)--(0,0), thickpen);  draw((-0.865,0)--(-0.4325,-0.75), thickpen); draw((0.865,0)--(0.4325,-0.75), thickpen);  // dots dot((0,1.5)); dot((-0.4325,0.75)); dot((0.4325,0.75)); dot((-0.865,0)); dot((0.865,0)); dot((-1.2975,-0.75)); dot((1.2975,-0.75)); dot((-0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0.4325,-0.75)); dot((0,0));  // center circles label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0,1)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(-0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0.865,-0.5)); label(&quot;$\\circ$&quot;,(0, -1)); [/asy]\" width=\"262\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>As shown, each of the small equilateral triangles are inscribed in the small circles. Since we know the radius of the circle, using the formula for equilateral triangle inscribed in a circle, the area of the small equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/599d51764b4a56c5ccc5f4f862f8362db3a74c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" />. To solve the actual problem, you need to take the area of the equilateral triangle, and add it to the area of 1 small circle, subtracted from the area of 1 small equilateral triangle (all the other small arc figures cancel out, as shown in the past diagrams). The area of the large equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1fb66751f2ee2626c75303485dba473c39a2f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cdot$\" style=\"vertical-align: 3px\" width=\"3\" height=\"2\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/599d51764b4a56c5ccc5f4f862f8362db3a74c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" /> (Latter is area of each small equilateral triangle.) This equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cf5b74e742a2827a5222cde1414557fac976606.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{27}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"38\" />. We know the area of 1 small circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> (we found radius earlier), so subtracting the area of the small equilateral triangle gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add22d9bda21e86390bc74fa4dde17730a442da7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/599d51764b4a56c5ccc5f4f862f8362db3a74c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" />. Then adding this back to the large equilateral triangle (we already subtracted earlier), gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/6/34609f5d54c5cba5b4a5ffadab53dfda04bbcaaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{27}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2+\\pi-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"200\" height=\"38\" />. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/3/4139f1958c792cdd31be7744887acb10fcec2953.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{24}{4}\\sqrt{3}+\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />, and when simplified, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de8361167f0958e4c9ecb307d012f37a3ad8985c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt{3}+\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"68\" height=\"21\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/e/d5eb8726a34932d28103ce7ce17456421222603f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"9\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/4/a04defad649f5e80f180b51122b67a7f9559242f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\pi+6\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"113\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p>Small Alternate Solution - Since it is seen that there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> when we simplify the expressions above, the only answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/4/a04defad649f5e80f180b51122b67a7f9559242f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\pi+6\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"113\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p>~spacex_fanboy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A\">2012 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=